    ("Explain the Kelly Criterion to me like I understand basic probability but not much finance theory.",
     "The Kelly Criterion answers: given a bet with known odds, what fraction of your bankroll should you wager to maximise long-term growth?\n\nFormula: f* = (bp - q) / b\n\nWhere:\n- f* = fraction of bankroll to bet\n- b = odds received on the bet (net payout per dollar wagered)\n- p = probability of winning\n- q = probability of losing (1 - p)\n\nConcrete example: you find a coin that lands heads 60% of the time. Someone offers you even money (b = 1). Kelly says bet f* = (1 * 0.6 - 0.4) / 1 = 0.2, or 20% of your bankroll each flip.\n\nWhy it works: Kelly maximises the expected logarithm of wealth, which is equivalent to maximising the geometric growth rate. Bet more than Kelly and you risk ruin through variance. Bet less and you leave growth on the table. Bet exactly Kelly and no other strategy will outgrow you over the long run.\n\nThe catch for real investing: Kelly assumes you know the true probabilities. You never do. In practice, most quantitative investors use 'half Kelly' or 'quarter Kelly' to account for estimation error. Overbetting Kelly is catastrophic; underbetting is merely suboptimal. The asymmetry strongly favours conservative sizing.\n\nWarren Buffett and Charlie Munger have both described their approach as intuitive Kelly without the formula, concentrating capital in high-conviction positions while never betting enough to risk permanent loss."),

