    ("What's the most efficient way to do a rolling window aggregation in pandas on a 100M row DataFrame?",
     "At 100M rows, vanilla pandas `.rolling()` will work but you will be waiting. Here is the hierarchy from slowest to fastest:\n\n1. **pandas .rolling()**: works, single-threaded, pure Python overhead on custom functions. For built-in aggregations (mean, sum, std) it uses Cython internally and is tolerable.\n\n2. **polars .rolling()**: 3-5x faster than pandas due to Rust backend and automatic parallelism. If you can switch to polars, do it. `df.with_columns(pl.col('value').rolling_mean(window_size=100))` will saturate all cores.\n\n3. **numpy stride_tricks + vectorized ops**: for simple aggregations (mean, sum), create a strided view and use numpy operations. Zero copy, SIMD-optimized:\n```python\nfrom numpy.lib.stride_tricks import sliding_window_view\nwindows = sliding_window_view(arr, window_size=100)\nresult = windows.mean(axis=1)\n```\n\n4. **numba @njit with a manual accumulator**: for complex custom aggregations, write a single-pass O(n) algorithm with numba JIT. Avoid recomputing the full window each step; use an incremental update pattern.\n\n5. **cuDF (GPU)**: if you have a GPU available, cuDF's rolling operations on 100M rows complete in seconds. Same pandas API.\n\nFor your setup: since you do not have a CUDA GPU on the Mac Studio, I would go with polars for standard aggregations or numba for custom ones. Either will process 100M rows in under 30 seconds."),

    ("Can you write me a Python context manager that temporarily redirects all logging output to a file?",
     "```python\nimport logging\nimport contextlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Optional\n\n@contextlib.contextmanager\ndef redirect_logging(\n    filepath: str | Path,\n    level: int = logging.DEBUG,\n    logger_name: Optional[str] = None,\n    mode: str = \"a\",\n):\n    \"\"\"Temporarily redirect logging output to a file.\n    \n    Args:\n        filepath: Log file path\n        level: Minimum level to capture\n        logger_name: Specific logger (None = root logger)\n        mode: File open mode ('a' for append, 'w' for overwrite)\n    \"\"\"\n    logger = logging.getLogger(logger_name)\n    handler = logging.FileHandler(filepath, mode=mode)\n    handler.setLevel(level)\n    handler.setFormatter(logging.Formatter(\n        \"%(asctime)s %(name)s %(levelname)s %(message)s\"\n    ))\n    \n    # Save existing state\n    old_handlers = logger.handlers[:]\n    old_level = logger.level\n    old_propagate = logger.propagate\n    \n    # Replace handlers\n    logger.handlers = [handler]\n    logger.setLevel(level)\n    logger.propagate = False\n    \n    try:\n        yield handler\n    finally:\n        handler.close()\n        logger.handlers = old_handlers\n        logger.level = old_level\n        logger.propagate = old_propagate\n```\n\nUsage:\n```python\nwith redirect_logging(\"/tmp/debug.log\", level=logging.WARNING):\n    logging.warning(\"This goes to the file\")\n    do_noisy_operation()\nlogging.info(\"This goes back to normal handlers\")\n```\n\nThe context manager saves and restores the full handler state, so nested usage and exception handling work correctly."),

    ("How does the Linux OOM killer decide which process to kill?",
     "The OOM killer scores every process using `oom_score_adj` plus a heuristic based on memory consumption. The algorithm in `mm/oom_kill.c`:\n\n1. **Base score**: proportional to the process's resident set size (RSS) as a fraction of total available memory. A process using 50% of RAM gets a base score around 500 (out of 1000).\n\n2. **Adjustment**: each process has an `oom_score_adj` value from -1000 to +1000 (writable via `/proc/[pid]/oom_score_adj`). This is added to the base score. Setting -1000 makes the process immune. Setting +1000 makes it the first to die.\n\n3. **Selection**: the process with the highest combined score gets killed via SIGKILL. Children are killed first if they share the same memory (to free memory faster without killing the parent).\n\n4. **Special cases**: kernel threads, init (PID 1), and processes in a different memory cgroup are excluded.\n\nPractical implications for your setup: vMLX on the Mac Studio should have its `oom_score_adj` set to -500 or lower to protect it. On Linux (the DGX Sparks), the RAG server and embedding models should be similarly protected. The processes most likely to be killed are large, recently-spawned, non-essential ones, so a runaway data processing job would be targeted before a long-running server.\n\nOn macOS, there is no OOM killer. Instead, the kernel uses `jetsam` (memory pressure events) and will kill processes based on a priority system defined in `/usr/share/misc/jetsam_properties.plist`. Less controllable than Linux."),

